#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build a slice's act-keyed history as a Git repository.

Usage: act-history {check,graph,structure,plate,commonality,emit} [--source PATH]

Two kinds of slice, one shape. A missal's units are prayers standing in a Mass;
a code's units are canons standing in a division of the code. Everything else --
the station is an act, the parents declare what they rest on, lines never
rejoin, commonality is computed -- is the same for both, so a slice declares its
`vocabulary` and nothing else changes. See VOCABULARIES below.

The station is normally the act, never the book. A commit is a promulgation, a
revision, a reform, a rubrical code or a documented exemption, and carries the
instrument it cites; a printing is a witness and hangs beneath the history in
`witnesses/`. That is what keeps a scanner out of the record: two printings of
one prayer differ constantly and no act stands behind those differences, so none
of them can become a commit.

THE ONE EXCEPTION, and it is narrow. Before Trent there are missals for which no
act has been located at all, and refusing them would leave the record silent
about books that survive. So a station declares which kind it is:

  promulgated  an act stands behind it and its instrument is cited.
  printed      a missal survives and NO act is claimed. A weaker station, and it
               reads as one everywhere -- in `check`, in acts/, and in the commit
               subject, which is prefixed `[printed]`.

`printed` is not a synonym for `act_citation = "not-found"`. Not-found means an
instrument is believed to exist and nobody here has read it; printed means no
instrument is claimed. Two scans of one printing are one witness and never two
stations: a station is a distinct edition, never a rescan.

Four things this does that a linear generator cannot, and the last is the one
the shape exists for:

  MULTIPLE PARENTS   an act may apply a decree to a text, and both are parents.
                     A merge is a claim that a reception happened, so `check`
                     refuses any act with two parents that does not cite one.
  MULTIPLE BRANCHES  one ref per line. Lines never rejoin: no act may name
                     parents on two different lines, and nothing here can draw
                     a merge across them.
  PARALLEL STAYS PARALLEL  a fork is born at an act. Where a line's other side
                     has no witness in the corpus, the branch is a root and
                     nothing more, and its emptiness is the record.
  COMMONALITY IS COMPUTED  `commonality` answers "what did two lines share
                     before diverging" with `git merge-base` against the emitted
                     repository, and with a tree comparison of the two tips. No
                     table of commonalities is written by hand.

Commit dates are the acts' own dates, shifted forward ten thousand years. That
is not cosmetic and not inherited thoughtlessly: git 2.55 rejects a negative
timestamp outright -- `git fsck --strict` reports `badDate` and every date
format prints empty -- so a true 1570 date cannot be stored in a way any tool
will show. A shift small enough to look plausible would be read as a real date;
11570 cannot be mistaken for anything. The true date is in the tree, in the
commit message, and in an `Act-date` trailer, so nothing depends on the stamp.
"""
